Product Matching & Hierarchy

Senior Project 401 — PriceMatch

Project Overview

This module focuses on the Product Matching & Hierarchy Problem within the PriceMatch project.

The objective is to develop a system capable of cleaning, normalizing, categorizing, and matching product records collected from different online retailers.

Because retailers often use different naming conventions, product descriptions, and category structures, identifying identical products across multiple datasets presents a significant challenge.

This module investigates data preprocessing, product normalization, hierarchy-based filtering, and machine-learning techniques to improve the accuracy and reliability of product matching.

Project Objectives

Clean and prepare product datasets collected from different retailers.

Normalize product names, brands, model numbers, and category information.

Establish a consistent product hierarchy for comparing products.

Identify potential matching products across different stores.

Develop and evaluate a product-matching algorithm.

Measure matching performance using accuracy, precision, recall, and F1 score.

Main Components

1. Data Cleaning — data_cleaning.py

Responsible for preparing raw product datasets.

Planned operations include:

Detecting and handling missing values

Removing duplicate records

Validating product names and prices

Correcting inconsistent data formats

Preparing cleaned datasets for normalization

2. Data Normalization — data_normalization.py

Responsible for transforming cleaned product records into a consistent representation.

Planned operations include:

Standardizing product names

Normalizing capitalization and whitespace

Handling punctuation and special characters

Standardizing brand names

Extracting model numbers and relevant attributes

Standardizing category and subcategory identifiers

3. Product Matching and Machine Learning — matching_ml.py

Responsible for identifying products that represent the same item across different retailers.

Planned operations include:

Generating candidate product pairs

Applying category and hierarchy-based filtering

Calculating product-name similarity

Comparing brands, model numbers, and other available attributes

Training a supervised matching model using labeled product pairs

Classifying matching and non-matching products

Evaluating matching performance

The initial matching approach may combine rule-based techniques with machine learning.


Evaluation Metrics

The matching algorithm will be evaluated using manually labeled examples of matching and non-matching products.

Metric

Purpose

Accuracy

Percentage of correctly classified product pairs

Precision

Proportion of predicted matches that are correct

Recall

Proportion of actual matches successfully identified

F1 Score

Harmonic mean of precision and recall

Precision and recall will be especially important because product-matching datasets may contain substantially more non-matching pairs than matching pairs.

